====

Points this section needs to cover:

* What a worktree is: multiple working trees sharing a single repository
  (one object database, one set of refs, many checkouts)
* `git worktree add`, `list`, `move`, `remove`, `lock`/`unlock`, `prune`;
  `git worktree add --orphan` for empty starting points
* Use cases:
** fixing a bug on another branch without stashing or disturbing a work in progress
** running builds/tests on one branch while working on another
** one checkout per coding agent for parallel agent work
   (forward pointer to <<ch07-git-and-agents,Ch 7, Git and Agents>>)
* Rules and housekeeping: a branch can only be checked out in one worktree at a
  time; where the linked worktree metadata lives; cleaning up with `prune`
* New diagrams needed in `diagram-source/` (shared repository, multiple working trees)
